  "content": {
    "title": "Michigan vs UConn national championship fast facts, key matchups",
    "text": "April 5, 2026, 5:08 a.m. ETFree Press sports writer Christian Romo previews the NCAA men's basketball national title game between Michigan basketball and UConn, set for Monday, April 6, in Indianapolis.Michigan vs UConn fast facts in national title gameMatchup: Midwest 1-seed Michigan (36-3) vs. East 2-seed UConn (34-5); NCAA Tournament national final.Tipoff: 8:50 p.m. ET, Monday, April 6; Lucas Oil Stadium, Indianapolis.TV: TBS, truTV, TNT.Streaming: On Sling TV, HBO Max.Watch NCAA national title game on Sling TVMichigan basketball: Meet the WolverinesLocation: Ann Arbor.Coach: Dusty May (63-13 in two seasons at U-M; 189-82 overall in eight seasons).School tournament record: 73-31 in 33 appearances, nine Final Fours, one championship (1989).Past 10 games: 9-1.Scoring leaders: Yaxel Lendeborg, 15.2 points per game; Morez Johnson Jr., 13.2; Aday Mara, 11.8.Rebounding leaders: Johnson, 7.3 rebounds per game; Lendeborg, 7; Mara, 6.8.Assist leaders: Elliot Cadeau, 5.8 assists per game; Lendeborg, 3.3; Mara, 2.4.3-point leaders: Trey McKinney, 38.4%; Nimari Burnett, 38.1%; Cadeau, 37.7%; Lendeborg, 37.2%.Player stats as of Saturday entering Final Four.The buzz: Michigan is not only looking like the most dominant team in this NCAA Tournament, but possibly the most dominant team in any NCAA tournament – and no, that's not hyperbole.With a 91-73 win over 1-seed Arizona in the Final Four, Michigan became the first team in NCAA history to record five games of at least 90 points scored in a single tournament. The Wolverines currently have a plus-108 combined margin of victory in this tournament, already the sixth-highest in NCAA history with one game left to play.But there's no guarantee the Wolverines will continue their dominance against UConn.The biggest concern for the Wolverines over the next couple of days is the health status of Big Ten Player of the Year and first-team All-American Yaxel Lendeborg, who injured both his left knee and left ankle in a fall during the first half of the Arizona game. He toughed it out for spurts in the second half and finished with 11 points, but there's no guarantee Lendeborg will be effective on Monday even if he gets the green light to play. He said he would suit up \"not matter what goes on\" immediately on the TBS postgame on-court interview.That means the scoring for Michigan's biggest game of the season will fall to its role players, but that's not necessarily a bad thing. Michigan is rich with offensive talent, with center Aday Mara putting up a game-high 26 points against the Wildcats and point guard Elliot Cadeau shaking off a tough first half to finish with 13 points, 10 assists, five rebounds and four steals.Standout freshman guard Trey McKenney − the 2025 Michigan Mr. Basketball out of Orchard Lake St. Mary's − has been dynamic off the bench, and pitched in 16 points against Arizona while looking like a future Big Ten star.Even if Lendeborg can't contribute what he usually does, the Wolverines deep roster may still present a talent advantage over the Huskies. But UConn is in this spot for a reason, and it shouldn't surprise college basketball fans that it will compete for its third title in four seasons.Michigan is 1-6 all-time in the the national championship game.UConn basketball: Meet the HuskiesLocation: Storrs, Connecticut.Coach: Dan Hurley (199-74 in eight seasons at UConn, 350-179 overall in 16 seasons).School tournament record: 77-33 in 39 appearances, eight Final Fours, six championships (1999, 2004, 2011, 2014, 2023 and 2024).Past 10 games: 8-2.Scoring leaders: F Tarris Reed Jr., 14.7 points per game; F Alex Karaban, 13.2; G Soloman Ball, 12.9.Rebounding leaders: Reed, 8.8 rebounds per game; Karaban, 5.2; G Silas Demary Jr., 4.5.Assist leaders: Demary, 5.9 assists per game; G Malachi Smith, 3.0; Reed Jr., 2.4.3-point leaders: Smith, 46.7%; Demary, 40.5%; Karaban, 38.6%.The buzz: According to the rules of the NCAA Tournament, when the Huskies make it to the Final Four, they win.Okay, that's not actually a rule, but it might as well be. The Huskies have made the Final Four eight times in their history and have won a national title in six of those appearances, recording a 13-1 record in Final Four games over their history. This team has the pedigree to make it 14-1.The offense starts with point guard Soloman Ball, who started slow this tournament but picked it up with 13 points in the team's win over Illinois in the Final Four.Forward Alex Karaban had been one of the best players in the tournament before a 1-for-8 shooting performance against the Illini, but look for him to rebound, both metaphorically and literally, against the Wolverines.And don't forget about the 3-point heroics of guard Braylon Mullins, who hit one of the biggest shots in tournament history in the Elite Eight against Duke, and added a dagger late in UConn's Final Four win.Tarris Reed Jr. is a big presence inside and is quite familiar with the Wolverines: He spent his first two seasons at Michigan under then-coach Juwan Howard, before transferring in April 2024.Coach Dan Hurley has continued a recent winning tradition, winning back-to-back championships in 2023 (over San Diego State) and 2024 (over Purdue) to give the Huskies six in total. And even though the Huskies didn't come into the tournament as the favorite, they have been playing like another championship is inevitable, regardless if Michigan has the roster advantage.Need to catch up on the news during your lunch break? Sign up for our Sports Briefing newsletter to get daily summaries of Detroit sports! You can reach Christian at cromo@freepress.com.",
